        "original_id": "24929ba6-4fee-50f6-95a3-6cd0b3804960",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: Vanderbilt University, Emory University, Methodist Episcopal Church, Fugitive literary group\n- People: Cornelius Vanderbilt, John Emory, William Ridley Wills\n- Places: Oxford, Georgia, metropolitan Atlanta, Brownsville, Tennessee\n- Events: university foundings, relocation, naming, endowment\n\nFacts:\n- Emory College was founded in 1836 in Oxford, Georgia by the Methodist Episcopal Church and was named in honor of Methodist bishop John Emory.\n- Vanderbilt University was founded in 1873 and was named in honor of Cornelius Vanderbilt.\n- Cornelius Vanderbilt provided Vanderbilt University with its initial $1 million endowment despite having never been to the South.\n- In 1915, Emory College relocated to metropolitan Atlanta and was rechartered as Emory University.\n- William Ridley Wills was born in Brownsville, Tennessee on March 4, 1897.\n- William Ridley Wills graduated from Vanderbilt University and was a member of the Fugitive literary group.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
